# 5. COMMON & AGENT-SPECIFIC CONFIGURATION

Every agent must have two conceptual configuration layers.

## Layer 1 — Common Configuration

Defines shared settings and behavior that apply consistently across the architecture.

Examples:

- Identity reference.
- Configuration status.
- Supported contract version.
- Common execution preferences.
- General validation rules.
- Shared capability references.

## Layer 2 — Agent-Specific Configuration

Defines settings unique to an individual agent.

Examples:

- Agent-specific task preferences.
- Category-specific operational behavior.
- Agent-specific supported task types.
- Agent-specific capability references.
- Specialized response requirements.

The common framework must not force every agent to use identical configurations.

Do not create fake category workflows or capabilities.

Use the actual approved agent definitions and existing category mappings.

